{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>חֲמִשִּׁים צַדִּיקִים</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 18:24): ten for each and every city; 'הֲתַשְׁחִית בַּחֲמִשָּׁה' (would You destroy for five?) — nine for each and every city, and You, the Righteous One of the universe, would be counted with them; 'אוּלַי יִמָּצְאוּן שָׁם אַרְבָּעִים' (perhaps forty will be found there) — and four cities would be saved; and similarly thirty would save three, and twenty would save two, and ten would save one. And he did not request for fewer than ten, because the generation of the Flood had eight righteous people and they did not save their generation, and nine by way of combination he had already requested and did not find. All these are the words of the Rav, of blessed memory. \\n\\n And I am astonished: if so, what is this prayer and supplication with which he would entreat time after time — 'אַל נָא יִחַר לַאדֹנָי' (let not Adonai be angry), and 'הִנֵּה נָא הוֹאַלְתִּי' (behold now I have ventured)? For it is surely proper that forty should save four, and thirty and twenty should save according to the same reckoning, just as fifty would save five. And likewise what he said, that nine by way of combination he had already requested and did not find — but for forty-five he was seeking the combination, and there were not forty-five there, whereas nine perhaps might be found there! \\n\\n Now the opinion of the Rav is to say that the many are worthy of greater salvation than the few would effect even for a small salvation, as they said (Sifrei, Bechukotai): those who are few and do the Torah are not comparable to those who are many and do the Torah. And HaShem conceded that forty-five with the combination of the Righteous One of the universe would save all the cities as though they were fifty complete. From this point, when there are forty righteous saving four, even with the combination of the Righteous One, may He be exalted, they would be saved; and likewise thirty, and twenty, and ten, for He had already conceded this combination. And if you should say that He conceded it for forty-five, which are many, but perhaps He would not concede the combination for the few as we have said — the righteousness of HaShem is worthy to be combined and to save, since He conceded that He would not distinguish between many and few. This is the opinion of the Rav. \\n\\n But the way of the plain sense of Scripture is straightforward: he first said fifty in order to give a complete reckoning of ten for each and every one, and then went back and diminished as far as he could, and for saving them all he would have spoken. And I do not know who introduced to the Rav what he said."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ramban here refers to Rashi's comment which itself cites the Midrash; 'הרב' here refers to Rashi, who is the subject being disputed throughout. The Sifrei citation is from Bechukotai."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:31:41.224861370Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}